# Add to `crontab -e` on the PocketBase account.

# ─── Supervision keepalive — ONLY on a box without the user-systemd unit ───────
# The every-minute line is the crash-restart; @reboot is the boot-start.
# run-pocketbase.sh is a no-op when the process is already alive, so the
# every-minute tick is cheap. Skip this block on the live box — the per-user
# systemd unit (deploy/README.md §4) already supervises PocketBase there.

* * * * * $HOME/pb/run-pocketbase.sh >> $HOME/pb/cron.log 2>&1
@reboot   $HOME/pb/run-pocketbase.sh >> $HOME/pb/cron.log 2>&1

# ─── Nightly SQLite backup + 30-day prune (queue task M6) ─────────────────────
# WANTED ON EVERY DEPLOYMENT, systemd or not: once the curated movement layer
# lives in the DB (DEC-020) this dated .db is the only copy of it outside PB's
# own pb_data/. Runs 03:17 UTC nightly.
#
#   * cron turns an unescaped `%` into a newline, so every `%` in the command is
#     backslash-escaped: `\%F` reaches the shell as `%F` → data-YYYY-MM-DD.db,
#     one file per night.
#   * `.backup` is SQLite's online hot-copy — safe against the live DB; it
#     captures pb_data/data.db only (users + workouts + movements), not
#     auxiliary.db (request logs) or storage/ (uploaded files — none yet).
#   * needs `sqlite3` on the box. If it is missing, use PocketBase's built-in
#     Settings → Backups instead — see deploy/README.md, "Back up (SQLite)".
#   * a backup that never leaves the VPS does not survive the VPS: pair this
#     with the off-box pull on Chris's machine (same README section).

17 3 * * * sqlite3 "$HOME/pb/pb_data/data.db" ".backup '$HOME/pb/pb_backups/data-$(date -u +\%F).db'" >> "$HOME/pb/pb_backups/backup.log" 2>&1 && find "$HOME/pb/pb_backups" -name 'data-*.db' -mtime +30 -delete
